ofignorance.com", "url": "https://thetoolsofignorance.com/stats/player.php?id=huged01"}]}, "Edward Ambrose Hug made his MLB debut on July 6, 1903, with the Brooklyn Superbas, and it was also his only major league game. The appearance came in the second game of a doubleheader in Cincinnati. The sources describe him as a local amateur, and he entered in the fifth inning as a replacement for Lew Ritter. He drew a walk in his lone plate appearance, and the game was called in the seventh inning so the team could make a train out of town.\n\nHug was born July 14, 1880, in Fayetteville, Ohio, and died May 11, 1953, in Cincinnati. He shares the major league records for the shortest name and the shortest career. The Baseball-Reference Bullpen entry gives the same debut date and notes that he appeared in one game in 1903 for Brooklyn."], "durationSeconds": 4.021005375, "searchCount": 1}, "tool_result_meta": [{"id": "toolu_0179S6KoKKxvzXMd6JzmTdEB", "permission_decision": {"decision": "accept", "source": "hook", "reason_type": "hook"}}]}
